# Agent relay protocol

An autonomous relay of single-purpose agent sessions that works through a
plan of GitHub issues, one issue at a time. This file is agent-neutral: how a
session starts its successor, schedules itself and measures its budget is
defined by the runtime adapter for the agent in use
(`agents/runtimes/<agent>.md`, currently
[`claude-code.md`](runtimes/claude-code.md)).

Repository: `{{ github_owner }}/{{ project_slug }}`
Integration branch: `{{ integration_branch }}`

## Roles

- **Worker session:** implements exactly one issue and opens its PR, then
  starts the reviewer.
- **Reviewer session:** reviews that PR, fixes clear findings itself, merges,
  then starts the next worker.
- **Fallback session:** started twice a day by a scheduled task; restarts the
  relay if it stalled (>12h without a commit while issues remain open),
  otherwise stands down.

No session does two jobs. The work plan (which issues, in which order) lives
in `HANDOVER.md`, not in this file.

## Rules for every session

1. **Branches:** `{{ integration_branch }}` collects all reviewed merges
   (never force-push it). Each issue lives on its own feature branch
   `issue-NN-<short-slug>` off the integration branch. The default branch is
   only touched at the very end, by one PR the user reviews.
2. **Session start:** make sure the repository is checked out (see the
   runtime adapter), fetch and check out `{{ integration_branch }}`, read
   `AGENTS.md`, `agents/CODING_RULES.md`, this file and `HANDOVER.md`
   {%- if project_type != 'docs' %}, run `uv sync`{% endif %}, then do the
   one job your role defines.
3. **Commit early, push often:** commit and push after every completed unit
   of work. Git history and `HANDOVER.md` are the only handover channel —
   nothing outside pushed commits survives a session.
4. **Keep `HANDOVER.md` current:** update it before ending your session
   (done / in progress / next step / open PR and its state / pitfalls) and
   push it.
5. **Budget rule:** when less than 10% of your context budget is left (how
   to measure it: runtime adapter), start nothing new: commit and push,
   finalize `HANDOVER.md`, write an archive snapshot, and schedule the
   successor with a delay instead of starting it immediately (runtime
   adapter), then end the turn.
6. **Starting the successor:** follow the runtime adapter. The prompt must be
   fully standalone — use the templates below and fill in issue/PR numbers.
   Never end a session without having started or scheduled the successor,
   unless the relay is complete (rule 7). If starting fails in every way the
   adapter describes, record it in `HANDOVER.md`; the fallback picks the
   relay up within 12 hours.
7. **Completion:** when every issue of the work plan in `HANDOVER.md` is
   done, start no successor. Mark the relay as finished in `HANDOVER.md`,
   write a final archive snapshot (reason: "all issues done"), and ask the
   user whether to open the PR from `{{ integration_branch }}` to the default
   branch and disable the fallback task.

## Limits of unattended sessions — degrade gracefully

- Secrets and API keys are usually not available. Never call paid APIs, mail
  services or scraped websites for real; verify with tests that use fakes or
  mocks.
- Never invent user content (CV, credentials, personal data). Build the code
  path and document what the user has to provide.
- **Issues with acceptance items that cannot be verified in the session**
  (live run, scheduled workflow, test mail, user content): after merging, do
  NOT close the issue. Comment on it which items remain for the user and list
  them in `HANDOVER.md` under "Needs user / live verification". For the relay
  such an issue counts as done.

## Worker session (one issue)

1. Identify the next open issue N from `HANDOVER.md`; read issue N in full.
2. Create `issue-NN-<short-slug>` off the integration branch.
3. Implement the issue following `agents/CODING_RULES.md`; commit and push
   per unit of work.
4. Open a PR targeting `{{ integration_branch }}`. `Closes #N` does NOT
   auto-close issues for PRs into a non-default branch — the reviewer closes
   the issue manually after the merge.
5. Update `HANDOVER.md` (PR number, state: "review pending") and push it to
   the integration branch.
6. Start the reviewer session (worker → reviewer template) and end the turn.

## Reviewer session (one PR)

1. Review the full PR diff with fresh eyes. Three questions, in priority
   order:
   1. **Is the code as simple and as short as possible?** Flag
      overcomplication, speculative abstraction, unnecessary
      configurability, dead code, anything where 50 lines would do the job
      of 200.
   2. **Does hand-written code reimplement something an established package
      already provides?** Prefer the package; flag the reinvention.
   3. **Are the rules in `agents/CODING_RULES.md` followed?** Especially
      "Simplicity First" and "Surgical Changes".
2. Post the findings as a PR review with inline comments, most severe first.
   If there is nothing to flag, say so in a short review — do not invent
   findings.
3. **Fix only clear-cut findings yourself** (simplifications, package
   replacements, style or docstring fixes) and push them to the feature
   branch. **Structural or debatable findings are NOT implemented**: leave
   them as PR comments and record them in `HANDOVER.md`.
4. {% if project_type != 'docs' %}Run `uv run pytest` on the PR head; merge
   only if it passes. {% endif %}Merge the PR into the integration branch
   (regular merge, no force-push), close issue #N with a comment linking the
   PR (exception: unverifiable acceptance items, see above), delete the
   feature branch.
5. Update `HANDOVER.md` (issue N done, next issue) and write an archive
   snapshot (reason: "issue #N merged").
6. Start the next worker session (reviewer → worker template) and end the
   turn — or, if all issues are done, follow "Completion".

## Fallback session

Read `HANDOVER.md` and the state of the work-plan issues, then decide:

- All issues done or `HANDOVER.md` says the relay is finished: do nothing;
  end with one line suggesting to disable the fallback task.
- Latest commit on `{{ integration_branch }}` or an open feature branch is
  less than 12 hours old: the relay is healthy; end silently.
- Otherwise the relay stalled: adopt the role matching the recorded state
  (open PR with review pending → reviewer; otherwise → worker for the next
  open issue) and follow that role, including starting the successor.

## Handover archive

Snapshots of `HANDOVER.md` go to `docs/handovers/YYYY-MM-DD_HHMM.md`
(current UTC time), prefixed with one line
`# Handover <timestamp> — <reason>`. Write one after every merge, whenever
the budget rule triggers ("budget reached"), and at completion ("all issues
done"). Snapshots are append-only — never edit or delete old ones.

## Prompt templates

Worker → reviewer:

> Continue the autonomous relay on {{ github_owner }}/{{ project_slug }} as
> the REVIEWER session. Fetch and check out branch `{{ integration_branch }}`,
> read AGENTS.md, agents/RELAY.md, agents/CODING_RULES.md and HANDOVER.md.
> Review PR #<PR> for issue #<N> following the "Reviewer session" protocol:
> post a PR review (simplicity first, package reuse, coding-rules
> compliance), fix only clear-cut findings yourself, merge, close issue #<N>,
> archive the handover, then start the next worker session. Apply the budget
> rule.

Reviewer → worker:

> Continue the autonomous relay on {{ github_owner }}/{{ project_slug }} as
> the WORKER session. Fetch and check out branch `{{ integration_branch }}`,
> read AGENTS.md, agents/RELAY.md, agents/CODING_RULES.md and HANDOVER.md.
> Implement issue #<N> on a feature branch following the "Worker session"
> protocol, open the PR, update HANDOVER.md, then start the reviewer session.
> Apply the budget rule.

## Activation

Nothing in this file runs by itself. To start the relay:

1. Create the work plan as GitHub issues, each implementable from its own
   text alone (by hand, or with `agents/prompts/review_phase.md`).
2. Switch the relay on: `copier update --defaults --data agent_relay=active`.
   This marks the relay as active in `AGENTS.md` and creates `HANDOVER.md`.
3. Fill in the work plan (issue order) in `HANDOVER.md`, commit, push.
4. Create and push the integration branch:
   `git checkout -b {{ integration_branch }} && git push -u origin {{ integration_branch }}`.
5. Create the fallback task (`agents/prompts/fallback_routine.md`).
6. Start the first worker with `agents/prompts/kickoff_worker.md`.

To pause: disable the fallback task and let the current session finish. To
retire: merge the final PR, disable the fallback task, and run
`copier update --defaults --data agent_relay=dormant` (this removes
`HANDOVER.md`; its final snapshot stays in `docs/handovers/`).
